<p>In the latest turn in the Iran-Israel conflict, Israeli strikes hit Iranian nuclear-related and industrial facilities on March 27, including a uranium processing site in Yazd and the heavy-water complex at Khondab near Arak, as the war entered its fifth week. Iranian officials said the strikes caused no casualties and no off-site radiation leak from the targeted nuclear facilities. The International Atomic Energy Agency echoed that assessment, saying it had been informed of the attack on the Yazd yellowcake plant and had seen no reported increase in radiation levels. The attacks came amid a mix of military pressure and tentative diplomacy. Dawn reported that President Trump had extended by 10 days a deadline for Iran to reopen the Strait of Hormuz or face attacks on its civilian energy grid, while Secretary of State Marco Rubio said messages were still being exchanged even though Washington did not yet know who it would be dealing with on the Iranian side. Iran&#x27;s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said Tehran would exact a &quot;HEAVY price&quot; and argued that the strikes undercut the extended diplomatic deadline, while the Revolutionary Guards warned that industrial sites across the region were now potential targets. The United Nations, meanwhile, set up a task force to help keep shipping moving through Hormuz.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dOoZAX7H2S-XjviV8jGGcAcTj6sRFrsSMLgUSeH-uh1g0SI2EnMCNCod--Z4YJ8_kN7irVg72wD1VqIVVmgGmfXT6kUo6YRiu97OyhwuFaf8' alt='TEHRAN: Firefighters and rescue workers look for victims at a residential building, which was targeted by US-Israeli air strikes.—AFP' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Steel plants: Iranian officials said the strikes also hit Khuzestan Steel and Mobarakeh Steel, two of the country&#x27;s largest steel factories.</li><li>Regional threats: An Iranian vice president threatened on X to target Saudi Arabia&#x27;s Red Sea port of Yanbu and the Fujairah oil complex in the United Arab Emirates, raising concern well beyond Iran and Israel.</li><li>Lebanon warning: DW reported that the United Nations warned of a potential &quot;catastrophe&quot; in Lebanon if the conflict spread further across the region.</li><li>Workplace alert: Iran&#x27;s Revolutionary Guards told civilians working at industrial plants in the region to leave immediately after warning that those sites could be struck in retaliation.</li></ul><blockquote>HEAVY price for Israeli crimes - Abbas Araghchi</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>International Atomic Energy Agency: The watchdog said Iran had informed it that the Shahid Rezayee Nejad Yellow Cake Production Facility in Yazd province had been attacked, and it reported no increase in off-site radiation levels. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1986131/attacks-on-irans-n-plants-steelworks-belie-messaging'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iranian government: Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said the strikes contradicted the extended diplomatic deadline and that Tehran would make Israel pay a heavy price. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/27/israel-launches-strikes-on-iran-nuclear-sites-as-war-enters-fifth-week'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>US administration: Secretary of State Marco Rubio said message exchanges with Iran were continuing, but that Washington still did not know who it would be talking to on the Iranian side. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1986131/attacks-on-irans-n-plants-steelworks-belie-messaging'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iranian Revolutionary Guards: The IRGC warned that industrial sites in the region were now targets and told civilians at those facilities to leave immediately. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1986131/attacks-on-irans-n-plants-steelworks-belie-messaging'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1986131/attacks-on-irans-n-plants-steelworks-belie-messaging'>Attacks on Iran’s N-plants, steelworks belie ‘messaging’</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-is